Calculations and work

J (and J1), like glucose and pyruvate is composed of C, H, and O atoms. Calculation of the mass fraction left for H and O leads to the following variants depending on the number of C atoms:

- No O atoms possible in the case of 1 C atom;
- C₂H₄O could have been acetaldehyde and ethylene oxide, still none of these is an acid;
- C₃H₁₄O is impossible because the limit of alkyl moiety saturation exceeded;
- C₄H₂₄O is impossible because the limit of alkyl moiety saturation exceeded; C₄H₈O₂ is butyric acid (with butyrate J as its anion) with due regard to its unbranched character;
- C₅H₃₄O and C₅H₁₄O₂ are impossible because the limit of alkyl moiety saturation exceeded; C₅H₂O₃ is too unsaturated;
- J with 6 C atoms is impossible, since at least one C atom of glucose is found in CO₂;

A number of C atoms exceeding 6 is impossible due to J formation from one glucose molecule.

